Refuses to delete a type something still refers to, and writes nothing.

The reference is in another package, which is why the whole module is loaded
rather than just the paths named on the command line. want.error makes the test
assert the refusal and that every file came out untouched.

-- args --
delete struct Config

-- want.error --
type Config is still used at

-- go.mod --
module example.com/typedrefuse

go 1.22

-- cfg/cfg.in.go --
package cfg

// Config is referred to from another package.
type Config struct {
	Name string
}

-- app/app.in.go --
package app

import "example.com/typedrefuse/cfg"

func Name(c cfg.Config) string {
	return c.Name
}
